
    Arrays on Char, Pointers & Strings

 1. Array name = base address

- The array name gives the address of the first element.
- It behaves like a 'constant pointer', so you can't modify it.


#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};      // 4 x 5 = 20 bytes

    // a++;             // error
    // a = a + 1;      // error

    printf("a = %u\n", a);
    printf("&a[0] = %u\n", &a[0]);                               // same as a

    printf("a = %u  a+1 = %u\n", a, a + 1);                     // a+1 moves by 1 element (4 bytes): 1000 -> 1004

    printf("&a = %u  &a+1 = %u\n", &a, &a + 1);                // &a is the whole array's address, so &a+1 moves by 20 bytes: 1000 -> 1020

    return 0;
}


| Expression | Meaning                    | Jumps by |

|  a         | address of first element   | -        |
|  a + 1     | address of next element    | 4 bytes  |
|   &a       | address of whole array     | -        |
|  &a + 1    | address after whole array  | 20 bytes |

 2. Accessing elements

These are all the same thing:

a[i]  ==  *(a + i)  ==  *(i + a)  ==  i[a]
&a[i] == a + i


Example:

int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
printf("%d %d\n", *(a + 3), *a + 3);               // 40 13


- *(a + 3) gives the 4th element, which is 40.
- *a + 3 gives 10 + 3 = 13 (the * is applied first).

 Copy one array into another

b = a; is invalid. We have to copy one element at a time.


#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    int a[5] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}, b[5], i;

    // b = a;   // invalid, error

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        b[i] = a[i];

    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
        printf("%d ", b[i]);              // 1 2 3 4 5
    printf("\n");

    return 0;
}


 3. Working on an array with a pointer


 int pointer

int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}, i;
int *p;
p = a;

for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    printf("%d ", *p++);          // 10 20 30 40 50


Other versions (each starting with p = a):


printf("%d ", ++*p);   // 11 12 13 14 15  (increments the value at a[0], p doesn't move)
printf("%d ", *++p);   // 20 30 40 50 garbage  (moves p first, then reads; the last read goes past the array)


 char pointer

Reading an int array through a char * gives one byte at a time.


int a[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}, i;
char *p;
p = (char *)a;

for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)
    printf("%d ", *p++);     // 10 0 0 0 20


An int is 4 bytes, so 10 is stored as `10 0 0 0` (little endian). The pointer moves 1 byte per step, so 5 steps only cover the first 5 bytes.


 4. char array

A char array is just a collection of chars.


char a[] = {'a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e'};
printf("%d\n", sizeof(a));              // 5
printf("%d\n", a[1]);                  // 98 (ASCII of 'b')

a[1] ^= 32;                            // toggles the case bit
printf("%c\n", a[1]);                 // B  (ASCII 66)


 5. Strings

A string is a collection of chars **ended with `'\0'`**.


char s[] = "abcde";
printf("%d\n", sizeof(s));             // 6  (5 chars + '\0')

char a[] = {'a','b','c','d','e'};
printf("%d\n", sizeof(a));             // 5  (no '\0')

char a2[10] = {'a','b','c','d','e'};
printf("%d\n", sizeof(a2));            // 10

char s2[10] = "abcde";
printf("%d\n", sizeof(s2));           // 10 (abcde + '\0' + 4 more zeros)


### What is '\0'?

- Marks the 'end of a string'.
- Acts as a separator between valid and invalid chars in the array.
- Takes 1 byte, and all 8 bits are 0.
- It is not a printable character.

 ASCII values to remember

| Char | Value |

| space | 32 |
| '\0' | 0 |
| '\n | 10 |

 6. Scanning and printing strings

- To print a string we need an array.
- '%s' is used to scan and print.
- '%s' always needs an - address (the array name is already one, so no '&').


#include <stdio.h>
int main()
{
    char s[10];
    printf("enter a string:\n");

    scanf("%s", s);                // only accepts one word (stops at space)
    // scanf("%[^\n]", s);         // accepts the full line, including spaces

    printf("String is: %s\n", s);
    printf("%s\n", s + 2);        // prints from index 2 onwards

    // gets(s);                   // avoid it
    // puts(s);                  // prints a string

    return 0;
}


Input 'abc def' with  %[^\n] gets stored as
a b c ' ' d e f '\0' =  97 98 99 32 100 101 102 0.
Printing stops at the '0'.

Note: s[10] only holds 9 chars plus '\0'. Typing more than that overflows the array, so use 'scanf("%9s", s)' to be safe.


 7. Print a string char by char

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    char s[10];
    int i;

    printf("enter a string:\n");
    scanf("%s", s);

   // Wrong approach: sizeof(s)/sizeof(s[0]) gives 10, the array size, not the string length.
    // for (i = 0; i < 10; i++)

    for (i = 0; s[i] != '\0'; i++)         // correct
        printf("%c ", s[i]);

    // for (i = 0; s[i]; i++)             // same thing, compiler checks s[i] != '\0'

    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}


Output:

enter a string:
abc
a b c


 Key takeaways

- The array name is a constant pointer, so 'a++' isn't allowed.
- 'a + 1' moves by one element, '&a + 1' moves by the whole array.
- To copy arrays, loop element by element.
- A string needs a '\0' at the end, so  "abcde" takes 6 bytes.
- To loop over a string, check for '\0' instead of using sizeof.
    - gets() is unsafe (removed in C11), so avoid it.
